          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Formally, the
            %em Ricci curvature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ric
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ric
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            is a symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} rank-2 tensor
            obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+contraction"} contraction
            from the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ric
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ric(v, w)
            as the first order approximation of the infinitesimal behavior of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            spanned by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 6
                    %msub
                      %mi Ric
                      %mi jk
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mi O
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mn 3
                      %mo )
                    %mo ]
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi Euclidean
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d\mu _{g}
                  \\;=\;
                  \\left[1-{\tfrac {1}{6}}Ric_{jk}x^{j}x^{k}+O\left(|x|^{3}\right)\right]d\mu _{Euclidean}
          %p
            (where we are using the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+summation+convention"} Einstein summation convention
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            with vanishing Ricci curvature is also called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Ricci flat
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#Regularity Harmonic coordinate representation and regularity
          %p
            By a trick of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lanczos22"} Lanczos 1922
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DeTurckKazdan81"} DeTurck &amp; Kazdan 1981
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ric
                    %mi lm
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi lm
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi lm
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ric_{lm}
                  \\;=\;
                  \-\frac{1}{2}
                  \\sum_{j,k} g^{j k} \partial_j \partial_k g_{lm}
                  + Q_{lm}(g, \nabla g)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{j k}
            denotes the inverse of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+tensor"} metric tensor
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi lm
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{lm}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla g
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+function"} rational expressions
            in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerators"} numerators
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denominator"} denominator
            depends only on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mi det
                        %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\det g}
          %p
            Note that this formula describes the metric tensor as a quasilinear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+differential+operator"} elliptic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PDE"} PDE
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                There are theorems that give bounds on the regularity of the metric tensor in harmonic coordinates under geometric assumptions (
                %a{:href => "#Anderson90"}> Anderson 1990
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#AndersonCheeger92"} Anderson &amp; Cheeger 1992
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#CheegerNaber13"} Cheeger &amp; Naber 2013
            %li
              %p As this expression is a quasilinear elliptic PDE, one can conclude on regularity bounds for the metric tensor from regularity estimates for the Ricci tensor.
          %p
            This argument allows for a regularity bootstrap in case of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+manifold"} Einstein manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            derivatives, the regularity theory for quasilinear PDEs gives
            = succeed "-regularity" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k+2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi Ric
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = \lambda Ric
            implies the same regularity for the Ricci tensor. Hence one can apply the argument again and add infinitum.
          %h3#cheegergromoll_theorem Cheeger-Gromoll theorem
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cheeger-Gromoll+theorem"} Cheeger-Gromoll theorem
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          #RicciTensorOfRoundNSphere.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}_{\gt 0}
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mo &gt;
                          %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/round+sphere"} round
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-sphere
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              satisfies
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ric
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ric(v,v) \;=\; \frac{n-1}{r^2}
            %p
              for all unit-length
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vectors"} tangent vectors
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T S^n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\vert v \vert} = 1
            %p
              Accordingly, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+curvature"} scalar curvature
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/round+sphere"} round
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-sphere
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
              with value
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} R
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{R} \;=\; \frac{n(n-1)}{r^2}
                    \\,.
            %p
          (e.g.
          = succeed ")" do
            %a{:href => "#Lee18"} Lee 2018, Cor. 11.20
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                    in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ricci+curvature"} Ricci curvature
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+curvature"} scalar curvature
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sectional+curvature"} sectional curvature
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-curvature"} p-curvature
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations+of+motion"} Einstein equations of motion
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+manifold"} Einstein manifold
          %h2#references References
          %p
            See most references listed at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
          %ul
            %li#Lee97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+M.+Lee"}> John M. Lee
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Riemannian manifolds. An introduction to curvature
                %strong 176
                Springer (1997) &amp;lbrack;ISBN: 0-387-98271-X&amp;rbrack;
              %p second edition (retitled):
              %p#Lee18
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+M.+Lee"}> John M. Lee
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricci_curvature"} Ricci curvature
          %p On Lanczos’s trick:
          %ul
            %li#Lanczos22
              %p
                Lanczos,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Ein vereinfachendes Koordinatensystem für die Einsteinschen Gravitationsgleichungen
                %strong 23
                (1922) 537-539
            %li#DeTurckKazdan81
              %p
                Dennis DeTurck, Jerry Kazdan,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some regularity theorems in Riemannian geometry
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=ASENS_1981_4_14_3_249_0"} numdam:ASENS_1981_4_14_3_249_0
          %p On regularity results:
          %ul
            %li#Anderson90
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+T.+Anderson"}> Michael T. Anderson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Convergence and rigidity of manifolds under Ricci curvature bounds
                %strong 102
                (1990) 429-445 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01233434"}> doi:10.1007/BF01233434
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF01233434.pdf"} pdf
            %li#CheegerNaber13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeff+Cheeger"}> Jeff Cheeger
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Naber"} Aaron Naber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lower bounds on Ricci curvature and quantitative behavior of singular sets
                %strong 191
                (2013) 321–339 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.1819"}> arXiv:1103.1819
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-012-0394-3"} doi:10.1007/s00222-012-0394-3
          %p For weaker but more general regularity results see also:
          %ul
            %li#AndersonCheeger92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+T.+Anderson"}> Michael T. Anderson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeff+Cheeger"} Jeff Cheeger
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\alpha
                \-compactness for manifolds with Ricci curvature and injectivity radius bounded below
              J. Diff. Geo.
              %strong 35
              2 (1992) 265-281 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-35/issue-2/Calpha-compactness-for-manifolds-with-Ricci-curvature-and-injectivity-radius/10.4310/jdg/1214448075.full"}> doi:10.4310/jdg/1214448075
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            A conjecture that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            Ricci flat manifolds either have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+holonomy"} special holonomy
            or else are “unstable”:
          %ul
            %li#Acharya19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bobby+Acharya"}> Bobby Acharya
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Supersymmetry, Ricci Flat Manifolds and the String Landscape
              %strong 2020
              128 (2020) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.06886"}> arXiv:1906.06886
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP08(2020)128"} doi:10.1007/JHEP08(2020)128
        .revisedby
